    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "s1_what",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "What a bacteriophage is",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Definition",
        "margin_note": "Definition, which phages are T-even, and the shape. Five lines.",
        "lines": [
          "Viruses that attack BACTERIA are called",
          "BACTERIOPHAGES, or phages.",
          "T-even phages are the T2, T4 and T6 phages that",
          "infect Escherichia coli.",
          "A bacteriophage is TADPOLE SHAPED: a large head",
          "and a narrow tail."
        ],
        "why": "The head holds the genome and the tail delivers it, so the tadpole shape is a division of labour rather than a coincidence.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying a bacteriophage is a bacterium. It is a VIRUS that attacks bacteria.",
          "Leaving out the shape. Tadpole shaped is the phrase that carries this mark."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "A virus that eats bacteria, shaped like a tadpole."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_head",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "The head",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Head",
        "margin_note": "Shape, what it is made of, what it holds, and the collar.",
        "lines": [
          "The HEAD is HEXAGONAL and is capped by a",
          "hexagonal pyramid.",
          "It is made of protein capsomeres.",
          "The head encloses the phage's DNA, which is",
          "double stranded and tightly coiled.",
          "The head is joined to the tail by a COLLAR."
        ],
        "why": "The head is only a container: everything it does at the moment of infection is done by what is packed inside it.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying the head is round. It is HEXAGONAL, capped by a pyramid.",
          "Saying the head contains RNA. T-even phage DNA is double stranded DNA."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "A six sided box with a pointed cap, full of DNA."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_tail",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "The tail",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Tail",
        "margin_note": "All three parts of the tail, then how it works. The syringe comparison is worth one line.",
        "lines": [
          "The TAIL is made of:",
          "1. a hollow CORE, down the middle;",
          "2. a contractile SHEATH around the core;",
          "3. a BASE PLATE at the far end.",
          "The tail injects the viral DNA into the host",
          "cell.",
          "The sheath contracts and drives the core",
          "through the bacterial cell wall, like the",
          "plunger of a syringe."
        ],
        "why": "A contracting sheath around a rigid core is a syringe, which is exactly the job the tail has to do.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Saying the whole phage enters the bacterium. Only the DNA is injected; the coat stays outside.",
          "Leaving out the core. The DNA travels down the HOLLOW CORE, not down the sheath."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "The sheath is the plunger, the core is the needle."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_plate",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "The base plate and fibres",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Base plate",
        "margin_note": "Plate shape, both counts, and what each part does.",
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Six fibres find the host; the tail does the",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          {
            "text": "injecting",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "At the tip of the tail is a HEXAGONAL BASE",
          "PLATE.",
          "It bears SIX TAIL PINS and SIX TAIL FIBRES.",
          "The tail fibres attach the virus to specific",
          "receptor sites on the bacterial cell wall.",
          "The pins hold the phage in place while the DNA",
          "is injected."
        ],
        "why": "The fibres decide which bacterium the phage can infect, because they only stick to matching receptor sites — which is why each phage attacks one host.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Giving the wrong count. There are SIX tail pins and SIX tail fibres.",
          "Saying the fibres inject the DNA. The FIBRES attach; the tail CORE injects."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Six and six: pins to grip, fibres to find."
      },
